-- title:
--   The path space $\mathbb D(\mathbf X)$
-- statement:
--   Given a separable metric space $\mathbf X$, $\mathbb D(\mathbf X)$ is the set of RCLL paths from $[0,\infty)$ to $\mathbf X$, and $\mathcal B(\mathbb D(\mathbf X))$ is its Borel $\sigma$-algebra.
--
--   It carries the laws of Markov processes and their couplings.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathcal B(\mathbb D(\mathbf X))$ for the $J_1$ topology on a separable metric space is the $\sigma$-algebra generated by the coordinate maps, and this is the formal definition.
-- source:
--   Lipshutz, Ramanan, Sensitivity analysis for the stationary distribution of RBM, arXiv:1907.00472v2, https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.00472v2, §8.1, p.33; §1.4, p.4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RBMSensitivity_IsRCLL
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped NNReal ENNReal RealInnerProductSpace

namespace RBMSensitivity

/-- The space `D(X)` of RCLL paths in `S`, with its σ-algebra: arXiv:1907.00472v2, §1.4, p.4, §8.1, p.33.

**Formalization Note.** The paper uses the Borel σ-algebra of the Skorokhod `J_1` topology. For a
separable metric space that σ-algebra is the one generated by the coordinate maps, which is used here;
the `J_1` topology itself plays no role in Theorem 8.1. -/
def Cadlag (S : Type*) [TopologicalSpace S] : Type _ := {ζ : ℝ≥0 → S // IsRCLL ζ}

instance Cadlag.instMeasurableSpace (S : Type*) [TopologicalSpace S] [MeasurableSpace S] :
    MeasurableSpace (Cadlag S) :=
  ⨆ t : ℝ≥0, MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ζ : Cadlag S => ζ.1 t) inferInstance

end RBMSensitivity


